//? Title: Median filtering
//? Subtitle: Actual replicated-border samples, seeded impulse noise and feature loss
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A median filter replaces each image pixel with a local middle-ranked value,
suppressing isolated impulses without averaging their magnitudes into
neighbours. We expose the actual samples selected around a tiny image, retain
the original seeded40-square edge/noise experiment, and measure both residual
extremes and disagreement with the clean image. Narrow structures can disappear,
so noise suppression is not a guarantee that every edge or feature survives.
)

\toc

> Derive the local rank objective

A radius r square window has W=(2r+1)^2 samples, with offsets −r..r in both
directions. Clamp each source index to the image boundary independently. Border
pixels therefore repeat: replicated extension gives those repeated values real
multiplicity in the local rank. Every output reads the original input, so the
method is a simultaneous nonlinear filter, not a sequential in-place smoothing
scan.

If the sorted local samples are v[0]..v[W−1], the median is v[W/2]. For an odd
number of samples, a median minimizes the sum of absolute deviations: moving the
candidate right increases each left sample’s distance and decreases each right
sample’s distance. Below the middle, more samples pull right; above it, more
pull left. This local L1 property explains why a single arbitrarily large
impulse can be rejected without averaging its magnitude into the output. It does
not prove restoration of an unknown clean image.

The producer uses nth_element to select the middle without fully sorting. The
trace records actual pre-selection sample reads and the actual selected value,
not a fictional sequence of sorting operations. Comparator calls count the
actual standard-library execution; exact counts can differ across
implementations.

> A tiny impulse and real border multiplicities

The3-square image has eight100 values and one central255 impulse. Its central
radius-one median is100. The actual corner neighbourhood repeats the original
top-left pixel four times; the table exposes every clamped source address read
for that corner. The diagram links the real central sample span and selected
result.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/median_filter_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/median_filter.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto t =
        dm::algorithms::median_filter_trace({{100, 100, 100}, {100, 255, 100},
                                             {100, 100, 100}});
    auto &corner = t.pixels.front();
    auto &centre = t.pixels[4];
    std::print("| actual corner sample | source row | source column | actual value |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: " "| ---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t k = corner.first; k < corner.first + corner.count; ++k) {
        auto &s = t.samples[k];
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", k, s.source_row, s.source_col,
                   s.value);
    }
    std::print("\nActual centre selected median: {}.\n", centre.median);
    dm::graphics::Diagram d;
    d.node("input", {0, 0},
           std::format("Actual central impulse\nvalue {}", t.input[1][1])).node(
        "window", {6, 0},
        std::format("Actual central window\n{} sampled values", centre.count)).node("median", {12, 0}, std::format("Actual selected middle\nvalue {}", centre.median)).edge("input", "window").edge("window", "median");
    d.save_svg("build/median_filter_tiny.svg");
}
```
