> A feature-loss control and checked execution

A one-column255 stripe in a zero image occupies only three of a radius-one
window’s nine samples, so its median becomes zero. A clean broad60/200 vertical
step is preserved under this particular aligned radius-one neighbourhood. These
controls distinguish an observed edge-preservation case from the false claim
that a median preserves every structure.

All int values are allowed;0..255 is a fixture convention. Negative radius and
ragged rows throw before indexing, including malformed empty shapes. Complete
window, output and sample geometry is checked before allocation. Radius zero is
the identity; empty or rectangular zero-width images retain their row shape
without sample reads. Difference summaries widen int subtraction before absolute
value, so INT_MIN versus INT_MAX is safe.

For P pixels and W=(2r+1)^2 samples per window, actual collection is PW.
nth_element has average linear work per window; no worst-case PW claim is made
for every standard library implementation. Workspace is O(W), plus O(P) returned
output. This implementation rebuilds each window and is not an incremental
sliding-histogram filter.

Optional complete capture uses3P+PW logical entries: input and output scalar
cells, one actual pixel record each and every actual sample record. Default cap
is one million; zero disables only that optional cap. Row headers, fields,
capacities, runtime workspace and bytes are excluded. Complete admission
precedes copying/evaluation. It is not a byte budget or a timing guarantee.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/median_filter_controls.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/median_filter.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    std::vector<std::vector<int>> step(5, std::vector<int>(5)), thin = step;
    for (int i = 0; i < 5; ++i)
        for (int j = 0; j < 5; ++j) {
            step[i][j] = j < 2 ? 60 : 200;
    thin[i][j] = j == 2 ? 255 : 0;
        }
        auto a =
            dm::algorithms::median_filter_analyze(step), b =
            dm::algorithms::median_filter_analyze(thin);
        auto sa =
            dm::algorithms::median_filter_compare(step, a.image), sb =
            dm::algorithms::median_filter_compare(thin, b.image);
        std::print("| prescribed clean fixture | actual changed pixels | actual samples | actual output pixels " "|\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| broad aligned step | {} | {} | {} |\n| single column " "stripe | {} | {} | {} |\n",
                   sa.changed, a.work.samples, a.work.pixels, sb.changed,
                   b.work.samples, b.work.pixels);
        auto p =
            dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot({double(sa.changed), double(sb.changed)},
                                         {"broad step", "thin stripe"},
                                         "changed pixels");
        p.x_label("prescribed clean image").y_label("actual changed pixels after radius-one median").title("Clean controls: a thin feature can disappear").save_svg("build/median_filter_controls.svg");
}
```
